- The data generator includes negative fixtures, redaction, schemas, fingerprints, strict-mode outputs, readiness reports, tabletop artifacts, and post-incident diffing.

## What this can become as core technology

The compelling product direction is not "better markdown reports." It is an incident-policy decision layer:

- Inputs: normalized event telemetry, service-priority contract, trusted-source profiles, policy packs, endpoint inventory, approval matrix, rollback controls.
- Core: deterministic policy compilation, risk scoring, confidence scoring, action grouping, TTL/rollback planning, safety blockers, evidence packaging.
- Outputs: decision brief, operator queue, approval queue, blocked-action list, rollback clock, machine-readable plan, rollback plan, decision trace, audit bundle, redacted demo bundle.
- Future integrations: ticketing, chatops, SOAR, change management, network automation, app-edge policy, service catalog, CMDB, and incident analytics.

The defensible moat would come from the service-priority contract, policy-pack language, safety rules, post-incident feedback, and a growing library of realistic scenario packs. The implementation already has the skeleton of that moat.

